---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  width="wide"
  title="About"
  description="Platform and infrastructure engineer: Kubernetes, CI/CD, migrations, and the guardrails that make AI agents safe near production."
>
  <header class="page-head wrap">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      I'm a platform and infrastructure engineer. I take prototypes to production and keep them
      there.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="wrap reading">
    <article class="reading__body prose">
      <h2>What I do</h2>
      <p>
        I work across the stack — Go and TypeScript services, Kubernetes, CI/CD — but the
        through-line is turning something that runs on one machine into a platform other people can
        build on. Since 2024 I've done that as a freelancer, mostly for teams whose product had
        outgrown the way it was deployed.
      </p>

      <h2>The arc</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="/projects/aztecscan">Aztecscan</a> was scale and protocol churn: a block explorer for
        a privacy L2 that shipped more than ten protocol versions while I was there, on three live
        networks that ran different versions on purpose. I wrote about a third of the codebase and
        owned the CI/CD and production manifests.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/projects/okutama">The Grid</a> was the opposite problem: a pile of unmanaged Lambda
        functions and App Runner services with no environment separation. Rebuilding it as a
        Kubernetes platform, environment by environment, taught me more about sequencing a migration
        than any of the individual tools did.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before that, <a href="/projects/bot-busters">Bot Busters</a> and
        <a href="/projects/boloney">Boloney</a> were realtime games. In both I owned the match state
        machine — turns, timers, fairness under latency, and what happens when a player disconnects.
      </p>

      <h2>How I work</h2>
      <p>
        I write the decision down before I make it. That habit produced a 459-line CI/CD migration
        plan, a 373-line ingress migration plan, and an architecture decision record that compared
        four rejected options before recommending one. When I reviewed my own decision record, I did
        it in the same critical / important / suggestion taxonomy I'd use on someone else's pull
        request.
      </p>
      <p>
        I don't do big-bang cutovers. Every migration I've run kept the old path alive until the new
        one was proven: one service at a time, one environment at a time, and the deprecated manifests
        still in the repo months later as evidence. The ten-week gap between an Envoy cutover and
        removing nginx is the part I'm proudest of.
      </p>
      <p>
        Lately the question I keep working on is what an AI agent needs before it can be trusted near
        production. My answer so far: give it the codebase's conventions in writing, give it one
        governed path to company data instead of ad hoc access, and put a human confirmation in front
        of anything irreversible. I built the first two and worked within the third, and I keep
        seeing the same pattern.
      </p>

      <h2>Availability</h2>
      <p>
        Open to freelance platform and infrastructure work. That usually means Kubernetes and CI/CD, a
        service split or migration, or making a deployment story that a small team can actually
        operate.
      </p>

      <h2>CV and contact</h2>
      <p>
        The full CV is available as <a href="/cv.pdf">PDF</a> or <a href="/cv.md">Markdown</a>. Email
        is the fastest way to reach me, and I'm on GitHub and LinkedIn.
      </p>
      <div class="about-links">
        <a href="mailto:sooth.dev@gmail.com">sooth.dev@gmail.com</a>
        <a href="https://github.com/Mautjee">GitHub</a>
        <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/mauro-eijsenring/">LinkedIn</a>
        <a href="/cv.pdf">CV (PDF)</a>
      </div>
    </article>

    <aside class="rail" aria-label="At a glance">
      <div class="rail__row">
        <span class="rail__label">Role</span>
